src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:54:        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">Yahoo Finance names Kevin Hassett the director of the National Economic Council.<Cite s="yahoo-hassett-august-jobs" passage="Kevin Hassett, the director of the National Economic Council" /> He told reporters outside the White House on October 2, &ldquo;What's happened is because we've reduced federal employment by 300,000 workers, then the numbers, uh, are a little bit lower if you don't account for that.&rdquo;<Cite s="hassett-briefing-2026-10-02" passage="What's happened is because we've reduced federal employment by 300,000 workers, then the numbers, uh, are a little bit lower if you don't account for that." /> Federal employment was {jobs(federalEmployment.december2024 * 1000)} in December 2024<Cite s="fred-federal-employment-2026-10-05" passage="2024-12-01,3009" /> and {jobs(federalEmployment.september2026 * 1000)} in September 2026,<Cite s="fred-federal-employment-2026-10-05" passage="2026-09-01,2682" /> down {jobs(Math.abs(federalEmploymentChange.sinceDecember2024) * 1000)}. September's own change was a drop of {jobs(Math.abs(federalEmploymentChange.september) * 1000)}, from {jobs(federalEmployment.august2026 * 1000)} in August.<Cite s="fred-federal-employment-2026-10-05" passage="2026-08-01,2683" /> Reuters wrote that government payrolls contracted {jobs(Math.abs(establishment.government) * 1000)}, mostly in local government, excluding education.<Cite s="reuters-september-jobs" passage="Government payrolls contracted 17,000, mostly in local government, excluding education." /></p>
src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:56:          <summary>Federal employment since December 2024</summary>
src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:57:          <p>The federal series counts jobs in thousands. December 2024 minus September 2026 is {federalEmployment.december2024} minus {federalEmployment.september2026}, a fall of {Math.abs(federalEmploymentChange.sinceDecember2024)} thousand. August minus September is {federalEmployment.august2026} minus {federalEmployment.september2026}, a fall of {Math.abs(federalEmploymentChange.september)} thousand. The series is seasonally adjusted federal government employment.</p>
src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:60:        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="4">The establishment survey counts jobs on employer payrolls. The household survey counts people. A jobs number and a people number can move differently in the same month. The payroll count comes from the first survey, and the unemployment rate from the second.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="The household survey measures labor force status, including unemployment, by demographic characteristics. The establishment survey measures nonfarm employment, hours, and earnings by industry." /></p>
src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:61:        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">The Federal Reserve's two goals are maximum employment and price stability.<Cite s="fomc-longer-run-goals-2025" passage="maximum employment and price stability" /> On September 16 the Federal Open Market Committee<Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" passage="The Federal Open Market Committee approved the following statement for release by a 12 – 0 vote:" /> raised the target range for the federal funds rate, its primary means of adjusting the stance of monetary policy,<Cite s="fomc-longer-run-goals-2025" passage="primary means of adjusting the stance of monetary policy is through changes in the target range for the federal funds rate" /> by 1/4 percentage point.<Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" passage="The Committee decided to raise the target range for the federal funds rate by 1/4 percentage point to 3-3/4 to 4 percent" /> The increase was in support of those goals.<Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" passage="in support of the Federal Reserve's dual mandate" /> This report is the employment reading before that committee meets on October 27-28; the next one, for October, is scheduled for November 6.<Cite s="fomc-calendar-2026-09-16" passage="October 27-28" /><Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="The Employment Situation for October 2026 is scheduled to be published on Friday, November 6, 2026" /> On prices, the personal consumption expenditures price index for August, whose annual change is how the committee measures inflation,<Cite s="fomc-longer-run-goals-2025" passage="as measured by the annual change in the price index for personal consumption expenditures" /> was up {admitted.pceAugust} percent from a year earlier.<Cite s="bea-pce-2026-08" passage="From the same month one year ago, the PCE price index for August increased 3.4 percent." /> This report put average hourly earnings up 3.0 percent over the past 12 months.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="Over the past 12 months, average hourly earnings have increased by 3.0 percent" /></p>
src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:62:        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">Reuters wrote that the slowdown was &ldquo;almost taking another interest rate hike from the Federal Reserve this month off the table&rdquo;.<Cite s="reuters-september-jobs" passage="almost taking another interest rate hike from the Federal Reserve this month off the table" /> The same dispatch said financial markets initially lowered bets on a rate hike at the October 27-28 meeting to {yardsticks.hikeOddsLow} percent before lifting them to about {yardsticks.hikeOddsLater} percent, little changed from Thursday, CME Group's FedWatch tool showed.<Cite s="reuters-september-jobs" passage="Financial markets initially lowered bets on a rate hike at the US central bank's October 27-28 meeting to 13% before lifting them to about 23%, little changed from Thursday, CME Group's FedWatch tool showed." /></p>
src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:113:        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">USA Today headlined the release &ldquo;September jobs report shows slowdown in hiring, unemployment ticks up&rdquo;.<Cite s="usatoday-september-jobs" passage="September jobs report shows slowdown in hiring, unemployment ticks up" /> The {jobs(septemberPayrollChange * 1000)} is a net change in payroll jobs, not a count of hires, and it is under about {jobs(establishment.significanceThreshold * 1000)}, the monthly change the bureau says is statistically significant.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="An over-the-month employment change of about 122,000 is statistically significant in the establishment survey" /><Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="Total nonfarm payroll employment changed little in September (+29,000)" /> Hires, &ldquo;all additions to the payroll&rdquo; during the month, are counted in a separate survey, and September's count is scheduled for November 3.<Cite s="bls-jolts-2026-08" passage="all additions to the payroll" /><Cite s="bls-jolts-2026-08" passage="The Job Openings and Labor Turnover news release for September 2026 is scheduled to be published on Tuesday, November 3, 2026, at 10:00 a.m. (ET)." /></p>
src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:114:        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">The rounded unemployment rate rose from {admitted.augustUnemployment} to {household.unemploymentRate}.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="Unemployment rate | 4.4 | 4.1 | 4.1 | 4.2 | 0.1" /> The bureau's summary says payrolls and that rate both &ldquo;changed little&rdquo;.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="Both nonfarm payroll employment (+29,000) and the unemployment rate (4.2 percent) changed little in September" /></p>
src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:126:          <p>September's 29,000 followed an average monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months, the figure the release states. Its three-month average line puts July through September at 51,000, and the same line a year earlier at 23,000.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="following an average monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months" /><Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="Total nonfarm | 23 | 28 | 51 | 51" /> The Associated Press said average job growth was an average 68,000 jobs a month so far this year,<Cite s="ap-september-jobs" passage="average 68,000 jobs a month so far this year" /> against its 9,700 average new jobs created every month in 2025.<Cite s="ap-september-jobs" passage="9,700 average new jobs created every month in 2025" /></p>
src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:133:          <p>Each month is that month's payroll total minus the month before, from the series saved October 3. The twelve months before September, September 2025 through August 2026, sum to 543 thousand and divide by 12 to 45,250. The release states that average as 45,000. The last twelve months include four declines: October 2025, December 2025, February 2026, and July 2026. October 2025, the first bar, is {october2025Levels.october} thousand minus {october2025Levels.september} thousand, a loss of {Math.abs(payrollChanges[0].change)} thousand. The 2026 average is January through September, 612 thousand divided by 9.</p>
src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:142:        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="6">The payroll figures for August and September are preliminary: the bureau says they rest on incomplete returns from the employers it surveys.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="estimates for the most recent 2 months are based on incomplete returns; for this reason, these estimates are labeled preliminary" /> The release states no cause for the {jobs(septemberPayrollChange * 1000)} change. Reuters wrote that economists noted payrolls &ldquo;have a tendency to underperform when the Labor Day holiday falls relatively late in September, as was the case this year.&rdquo;<Cite s="reuters-september-jobs" passage="Economists noted that payrolls have a tendency to underperform when the Labor Day holiday falls relatively late in September, as was the case this year." /> Whether hiring slowed is not known yet: hires are counted separately, August's hires were {admitted.joltsHiresMillions} million, and September's are scheduled for November 3.<Cite s="bls-jolts-2026-08" passage="Hires changed little at 5.2 million" /><Cite s="bls-jolts-2026-08" passage="The Job Openings and Labor Turnover news release for September 2026 is scheduled to be published on Tuesday, November 3, 2026, at 10:00 a.m. (ET)." /></p>

